00:04:05,910 --> 00:04:12,390
the problems that are in the componentry beneath you become the problems that are in the firmware, those problems become unresolvable.

38
00:04:12,390 --> 00:04:17,909
And they are deeply deeply frustrating and we just saw a bunch of them.

39
00:04:17,909 --> 00:04:19,509
Again, they were comical in retrospect.

40
00:04:19,509 --> 00:04:24,230
And I'll give you like a couple of concrete examples just to give you an idea of what kind of what you're looking at.

41
00:04:24,230 --> 00:04:31,030
One of the our data centers had really pathological IO latency.

42
00:04:31,030 --> 00:04:37,510
We had a very database heavy workload and this was kind of right at the period where

43
00:04:37,510 --> 00:04:43,670
you were still deploying on rotating media on hard drives.

44
00:04:43,670 --> 00:04:49,510
So this is like so an all flash by did not make economic sense when we did this in 2016.

45
00:04:49,510 --> 00:04:59,110
this probably be interesting to know like when was the kind of the last time that actual hard drives made sense cuz I feel this was close to it.

46
00:04:59,110 --> 00:05:08,150
So we had a a bunch of pathological IO problems but we had one data center in which the outliers were actually quite a bit worse and there was so much going on in that system.

47
00:05:08,150 --> 00:05:15,510
It took us a long time to figure out like why and because when you're IO you're seeing worse IO.

48
00:05:15,510 --> 00:05:19,190
I mean naturally you want to understand like what's the workload doing? trying to take a first principles approach. What's the workload doing?

49
00:05:19,199 --> 00:05:29,350
This is a very intensive database workload to support the object storage system that we had built called Manta and

50
00:05:29,350 --> 00:05:36,710
the metadata tier was stored in was we were using Postgress for that and that was just getting absolutely slaughtered and

51
00:05:36,710 --> 00:05:41,510
ultimately very IO bound with these kind of pathological IO latencies and

52
00:05:41,510 --> 00:05:46,310
as we trying to like peel away the layers to figure out what was going on and

53
00:05:46,310 --> 00:05:52,390
I finally had this thing it's like okay we are seeing at the device layer,

54
00:05:52,390 --> 00:05:59,830
at the disk layer, we are seeing pathological outliers in this data center that we're not seeing anywhere else.

55
00:05:59,830 --> 00:06:01,990
And that does not make any sense.

56
00:06:01,990 --> 00:06:11,029
And the thought occurred to me, I'm like, well, maybe we are do we have like different a different rev of firmware on our HGST drives?

57
00:06:11,029 --> 00:06:15,189
HGST now part of WD Western Digital were the drives we had everywhere.

58
00:06:15,189 --> 00:06:16,790
So maybe I had a firmware bug.

59
00:06:16,790 --> 00:06:20,469
had this not be the first time in my life at all that I would have a drive firmware issue.

60
00:06:20,469 --> 00:06:25,830
And I went to go pull the firmware rev and I'm like, Toshiba makes hard drives.

61
00:06:25,830 --> 00:06:33,189
So, I had no idea that Toshiba even made hard drives, let alone that they were they were in our data center.

62
00:06:33,189 --> 00:06:36,390
I'm like, what is this?

63
00:06:36,390 --> 00:06:46,150
And as it turns out, and this is, you know, part of the challenge when you don't have an integrated system, which not to pick on them, but Dell doesn't.

64
00:06:46,150 --> 00:06:55,909
And what Dell would routinely put just sub make substitutes and they make substitutes that they you know it's kind of like you're going to like I don't know Instacart or whatever and they're out of the thing that you want.

65
00:06:55,919 --> 00:07:07,029
So you know you're someone makes a substitute and like sometimes that's okay but it's really not okay in a data center and you really want to develop and

66
00:07:07,029 --> 00:07:15,029
validate an end toend integrated system and in this case like Toshiba doesn't I mean Toshiba does make hard drives but

67
00:07:15,029 --> 00:07:22,710
they are a or they did they basically were not competitive and they were not competitive in part for the reasons that we were discovering they had really serious firmware issues.

68
00:07:22,710 --> 00:07:29,350
So these were drives that would just simply stop acknowledging any reads for on the order of 2700 milliseconds.

69
00:07:29,350 --> 00:07:32,390
Long time 2.7 seconds.

70
00:07:32,390 --> 00:07:41,350
And that was a it was a drive firmware issue, but it was highlighted like a much deeper issue which was the simple lack of control that we had over our own destiny.

71
00:07:41,350 --> 00:07:48,870
And it's an example among many where Dell is making a decision that

72
00:07:48,870 --> 00:07:53,589
lowers the cost of what they are providing you marginally.

73
00:07:53,589 --> 00:07:58,710
But it is then giving you a system that they shouldn't have any confidence in because it's not one that they've actually designed.

74
00:07:58,710 --> 00:08:03,670
And they leave it to the customer, the enduser to make these discoveries.

75
00:08:03,670 --> 00:08:06,230
And these things happen up and down the stack.

76
00:08:06,230 --> 00:08:14,869
And for every for whether it's and not just to pick on Dell because it's it's true for HPE, it's true for super micro, it's true for your switch vendors,

77
00:08:14,869 --> 00:08:26,869
it's it's true for storage vendors where the one that is left actually integrating these things and trying to make the whole thing work is the end user sitting in their data center.

78
00:08:26,869 --> 00:08:34,949
There's not a product that they can buy that gives them elastic infrastructure, a cloud in their own DC.

79
00:08:34,949 --> 00:08:36,469
the product that you buy is the public cloud.

80
00:08:36,469 --> 00:08:45,590
Like when you go in the public cloud, you don't worry about the stuff because it's AWS's issue or it's GCP's issue and they are the ones that get this to ground.

81
00:08:45,590 --> 00:08:56,470
And they, and this was kind of, you know, the eye opening moment, not a surprise, they are not Dell customers, they are not HP customers, they are not super micro customers.

82
00:08:56,470 --> 00:09:01,190
They have designed their own machines and to varying degrees depending on which one you're looking at.

83
00:09:01,190 --> 00:09:11,190
But they've taken the clean sheet of paper and the frustration that we had kind of at Joy and beginning to wonder and then Samsung and kind of wondering what was next is that

84
00:09:11,190 --> 00:09:15,990
what they built was not available for purchase in the data center.

85
00:09:15,990 --> 00:09:19,269
You could only rent it in the public cloud.

86
00:09:19,269 --> 00:09:23,750
And our big belief is that public cloud computing is a really important revolution infrastructure.

87
00:09:23,760 --> 00:09:28,310
Doesn't feel like a different deep thought, but cloud computing is a really important revolution.

88
00:09:28,310 --> 00:09:31,110
It shouldn't only be available to rent.

89
00:09:31,110 --> 00:09:33,670
You should be able to actually buy it.

90
00:09:33,670 --> 00:09:35,190
And there are a bunch of reasons for doing that.

91
00:09:35,190 --> 00:09:46,949
One, and the one we saw at Samsung is economics, which I think is still the dominant reason where it just does not make sense to rent all of your compute in perpetuity. But there are other reasons too. There's security, there's risk management, there's latency.

92
00:09:46,949 --> 00:09:51,509
There are a bunch of reasons why one might want to own one's own infrastructure.

93
00:09:51,519 --> 00:10:01,190
But that was very much the the so the the genesis for oxide was coming out of this very painful experience and a painful experience that

94
00:10:01,190 --> 00:10:06,710
because I mean a long answer to your question about like what was it like to be at Samsung scale those are the kinds of things that

95
00:10:06,710 --> 00:10:15,350
we I mean in our other data centers we didn't have Toshiba drives we only had the HDSC drives but it's only when you get to this larger scale that

96
00:10:15,350 --> 00:10:23,350
you begin to see some of these pathologies but these pathologies then are really debilitating in terms of those who are trying to develop a service on top of them.

97
00:10:23,350 --> 00:10:33,269
So, it was very educational in that regard and you're very grateful for the experience at Samsung in terms of opening our eyes to the challenge of running at that kind of scale.

98
00:10:33,279 --> 00:10:44,630
And during that time at Joyant when you experienced some of these issues, was it more of a case of you didn't have enough servers experiencing this?

99
00:10:44,630 --> 00:10:51,509
So, if it would happen, you might say like, well, this one's not working, so maybe we'll just replace the hardware.

100
00:10:51,509 --> 00:10:56,710
What was the thought process when you were working at that smaller scale and and how did these issues affect you?

101
00:10:56,720 --> 00:11:00,069
Yeah, at the smaller scale you see fewer of them, right?

102
00:11:00,069 --> 00:11:02,630
You just see it's like, okay, we you know what you might see is like that's weird.

103
00:11:02,630 --> 00:11:07,269
We kind of saw this in one machine versus seeing it in 100 or a thousand or 10,000.

104
00:11:07,269 --> 00:11:09,750
So, you just see them less frequently.

105
00:11:09,750 --> 00:11:12,470
As a result, they are less debilitating.

106
00:11:12,470 --> 00:11:21,670
I think that it's when you go to that larger scale, those things that were unusual now become routine and they become debilitating.

107
00:11:21,670 --> 00:11:24,069
So it it really is in many regards a function of scale.

108
00:11:24,069 --> 00:11:33,030
And then I think it was also it was a little bit dispiriting that kind of the substrate we were building on really had not improved.

109
00:11:33,030 --> 00:11:44,470
And if you buy a computer server, buy an x86 server, there is a very low layer of firmware, the bias, the basic input output system, the UEFI bias.

110
00:11:44,470 --> 00:11:50,389
And this is like an abstraction layer that is has existed since the 80s and hasn't really meaningfully improved.

111
00:11:50,399 --> 00:11:59,190
But beyond that, like this lowest layer of platform enablement software is really only impeding the operability of the system.

112
00:11:59,190 --> 00:12:02,069
You look at the baseboard management controller which is the kind of the computer within the computer.

113
00:12:02,079 --> 00:12:16,870
There is an element in the machine that needs to handle environmentals that needs to handle operate the fans and so on and that traditionally has is the spaceport management controller and that architecturally just hasn't improved in the last two decades.

114
00:12:16,870 --> 00:12:25,110
And you know that's it's a proprietary piece of silicon generally from a company that no one's ever heard of called ASPE uh which has to be is written all in caps.

115
00:12:25,110 --> 00:12:28,069
So I guess it needs to be screamed.

116
00:12:28,069 --> 00:12:31,269
ASPE has a proprietary part.

117
00:12:31,269 --> 00:12:34,150
There is a root password infamously.

118
00:12:34,150 --> 00:12:37,430
The root password is encoded effectively in silicon.

119
00:12:37,430 --> 00:12:42,470
And for anyone who kind of goes deep into these things, like, oh my god, are you kidding me?

120
00:12:42,470 --> 00:12:46,790
When we first started Oxide, the Wi-Fi password was a fraction of the A-speed root password for the BMC.

121
00:12:46,790 --> 00:12:49,110
It's kind of like a little little BMC humor.

122
00:12:49,120 --> 00:12:55,829
But those things, it was just dispiriting that the state-of-the-art was still basically personal computers running in the data center.

123
00:12:55,829 --> 00:12:59,750
And that's part of what was the motivation for doing something new.

124
00:12:59,760 --> 00:13:03,829
And for the people using these systems,

125
00:13:03,839 --> 00:13:17,590
whether it's the baseboard management controller or it's the BIOS or UF UEFI component, what are the actual problems that people are seeing?

126
00:13:17,600 --> 00:13:24,630
Oh man, you're going to have like some fraction of your listeners, maybe a big fraction, are like, "Yeah, like what are the problems? That's a good question."

127
00:13:24,639 --> 00:13:32,629
And then you're going to have the people that actually deal with these things like their heads already hit the desk being like what are the problems? Like what are the non problems?

128
00:13:32,629 --> 00:13:37,509
Like what what works actually that's like a shorter answer.

129
00:13:37,509 --> 00:13:42,629
I mean there are so many problems and a lot of it is just like I mean there are problems just architecturally.

130
00:13:42,629 --> 00:13:48,950
These things are just so I mean the problems spread to the horizon so you can kind of start wherever you want but I mean as like as a really concrete example. Okay.

131
00:13:48,950 --> 00:13:56,069
Okay, so the the BMC's, the computer within the computer, that needs to be on its own network.

132
00:13:56,069 --> 00:13:58,230
So you now have like not one network, you got two networks.

133
00:13:58,230 --> 00:14:06,710
And that network, by the way, that's the network that you're going to log into to like reset the machine when it's otherwise unresponsive.

134
00:14:06,710 --> 00:14:09,829
So going into the BMC, you can are you're able to control the entire machine.

135
00:14:09,829 --> 00:14:14,069
Well, it's like, all right, so now I've got a second network that I need to manage.

136
00:14:14,079 --> 00:14:16,150
What is running on the BMC?

137
00:14:16,150 --> 00:14:24,470
what's running some ancient ancient version of Linux that you got it's like well how do I how do I patch that how do I like manage the vulnerabilities with that

138
00:14:24,480 --> 00:14:35,509
because if someone is able to root your BMC they control the system so it's like this is not and now you've got to go deal with all of the operational hair around that

139
00:14:35,509 --> 00:14:42,310
how do you upgrade that system updating the BMC is I mean it's like you've got this like second shadow bad infrastructure that

140
00:14:42,310 --> 00:14:52,550
you have to go manage generally not open source there's something called open BMC which people use to varying degrees, but you're generally stuck with the proprietary BMC.

141
00:14:52,550 --> 00:15:01,991
So, you're generally stuck with ISO from HPE or IDRA from Dell or the Super Micros BMC, the HP BMC. And it is just excruciating pain.

142
00:15:02,000 --> 00:15:05,750
This is assuming that by the way that everything is behaving correctly.

143
00:15:05,750 --> 00:15:16,710
the the problem is that these things often don't behave correctly and then the consequence of them not behaving correctly is really dire because it's at that lowest layer of the system.

144
00:15:16,710 --> 00:15:20,230
So I mean I'll give you a concrete example a customer of theirs reported to me.

145
00:15:20,230 --> 00:15:32,069
So I won't disclose the vendor but let's just say that a well-known vendor had an issue with their temperature sensors were broken and the thing would always read basically the wrong value.

146
00:15:32,069 --> 00:15:43,509
So it was the BMC that had to like invent its own different kind of thermal control loop and it would index on the actual inrush current.

147
00:15:43,509 --> 00:15:49,269
They look at that at the current that's going into the CPU to adjust the fan speed. That's an interesting idea.

148
00:15:49,269 --> 00:15:51,189
That doesn't work cuz that's actually not the temperature.

149
00:15:51,189 --> 00:15:55,430
So that software would crank the fans whenever you had an inrush of current.

150
00:15:55,440 --> 00:15:59,350
And this customer had a workload that would spike the current.

151
00:15:59,350 --> 00:16:05,189
And when it would spike the current, the fans would kick up and then they would slowly degrade over time.

152
00:16:05,189 --> 00:16:12,629
Well, this workload was spiking the current faster than the fans would degrade, but not fast enough to actually heat up the part.

153
00:16:12,629 --> 00:16:24,870
And ultimately, over a very long time, in a very painful investigation, this customer determined that like my fans are cranked in my data center for no reason.

154
00:16:24,870 --> 00:16:32,230
We're blowing cold air and it's like this is on the order of like 100 watts a server of energy that you shouldn't be spending and

155
00:16:32,230 --> 00:16:41,269
that comes down to this kind of broken software hardware interface at the lowest layer that

156
00:16:41,269 --> 00:16:46,389
has real meaningful consequence in terms of hundreds of kilowatts across a data center.

157
00:16:46,389 --> 00:16:51,509
So this stuff has very very very real consequence and it's such a shadowy world.

158
00:16:51,509 --> 00:16:59,350
Part of the reason that that your listeners that have dealt with this, their heads will hit the desk is because it is really aggravating to deal with problems at this layer.

159
00:16:59,350 --> 00:17:02,230
You feel powerless.

160
00:17:02,230 --> 00:17:04,390
You don't control or really see the software that's on them.

161
00:17:04,390 --> 00:17:07,429
It's generally proprietary.

162
00:17:07,429 --> 00:17:09,750
You're relying on your vendor.

163
00:17:09,750 --> 00:17:11,429
Your vendor is telling you that like, boy, I don't know.

164
00:17:11,429 --> 00:17:13,029
You're the only customer seeing this.

165
00:17:13,029 --> 00:17:22,470
I mean, the number of times I have heard that and I I have pledged that we're we're not going to say that at Oxide because it's such an unactionable thing to say like you're the only customer saying this.

166
00:17:22,480 --> 00:17:25,750
It's like it feels like are you blaming me for my problem?

167
00:17:25,750 --> 00:17:27,669
Feels like you're blaming me for my problem.

168
00:17:27,669 --> 00:17:40,549
And what you begin to realize is that to a degree these folks are speaking their own truth because the folks that are running at real scale at hypers scale those folks aren't Dell HP super micro customers.

169
00:17:40,559 --> 00:17:43,110
They are actually they've done their own thing.

170
00:17:43,110 --> 00:17:46,390
So it's like, yeah, the Dell's not seeing that problem because they're not running at the same scale.

171
00:17:46,390 --> 00:17:57,990
But when you do run, you only have to run at modest scale before these things just become overwhelming in terms of the headwind that they present to people that want to deploy infrastructure.

172
00:17:58,000 --> 00:18:01,430
Yeah.

173
00:18:01,430 --> 00:18:09,190
So maybe to help people get some perspective, at what point do you think that people start noticing or start feeling these problems?

174
00:18:09,190 --> 00:18:16,549
Because I imagine that if you just have a few racks or do you have a couple racks or

175
00:18:16,549 --> 00:18:28,870
the or you wonder just wondering cuz no no no I would think I think anyone who deploys any number of servers especially now especially if your experience is only in the cloud you're going to be like what the hell is this?

176
00:18:28,870 --> 00:18:33,909
I mean, just again, just to get this thing working at all, it's so hairy and so congealed, right?

177
00:18:33,919 --> 00:18:37,590
It's not designed.

178
00:18:37,590 --> 00:18:42,870
I mean, nobody who is setting up a rack of servers is going to think to themselves like, "Yes, this is the right way to go do it."

179
00:18:42,870 --> 00:18:44,630
This all makes sense.

180
00:18:44,630 --> 00:18:46,230
I mean, it's a bag of bolts.

181
00:18:46,230 --> 00:18:48,390
It's a bunch of parts that you're putting together.

182
00:18:48,390 --> 00:18:51,029
And so, even at the smallest scales, that stuff is painful.

183
00:18:51,039 --> 00:18:56,150
Just architecturally, it's painful at the small scale. But at least you can get it working.

